{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" one, it sat there `Available`, and a claim found it.\n\nThat model works, and it has a name, but it does not scale past a certain point, for a reason that becomes obvious the moment you try it. An administrator pre-creating PVs has to gu"}},"session_id":"381986e7-c44a-411d-a8fd-a174ddf25e17","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"f644faaa-8b83-43a3-abfb-ee73f4c1cac2"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"ess: how many, what sizes, what performance tiers, for which teams. Guess low and claims sit unbound. Guess high and you have provisioned storage nobody asked for. Guess wrong about the size distribution and you have forty 10Gi volumes and"}},"session_id":"381986e7-c44a-411d-a8fd-a174ddf25e17","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"fa0b7acb-325b-41ba-b164-28f3bfb8a1d9"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" a team that needs one 400Gi volume. It is stocking the hold for a manifest nobody has written yet.\n\nThe object that fixes this is the StorageClass, and it is the third element that has been sitting off to the side of the figure since §2.\n\n### What"}},"session_id":"381986e7-c44a-411d-a8fd-a174ddf25e17","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"9aa07288-761b-4bfc-bfa4-f29c4dae83c8"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" a StorageClass is\n\n*A StorageClass provides a way for administrators to describe the classes of storage they offer. Different classes might map to quality-of-service levels, or to backup policies, or to arbitrary policies"}},"session_id":"381986e7-c44a-411d-a8fd-a174ddf25e17","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"a0340858-8754-4ef3-9ce8-7ea2febe7de3"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" determined by the cluster administrators. Kubernetes itself is unopinionated about what classes represent* [source: k8s-docs-storage-classes-2026-08-25].\n\nThat last sentence is doing real work. Kubernetes does not know what `fast` means, or `"}},"session_id":"381986e7-c44a-411d-a8fd-a174ddf25e17","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"ef8a6e83-ace6-4c93-84b5-0d5fa2879b94"}
